    "narrative": "For this May 31 slate, **Corbin Carroll** (74.71) and **James Wood** (73.33) headline our offensive output. **Corbin Carroll** is a mandatory start against Bryce Miller; despite a slight park penalty, his elite sprint speed and aggressive order placement at #2 make him our premier asset for chasing scarce stolen bases. **James Wood** mirrors this utility, leveraging a favorable matchup against Canning and the premium #1 lineup spot to anchor our runs and RBI potential. We are also capitalizing on the favorable SP_xERA for **Vinnie Pasquantino** (73.64) against Leiter and **Brice Turang** (70.37) against Imai, both of whom benefit significantly from high-efficiency platoon advantages and prime spots in the order.\n\nThe most difficult exclusion was **Austin Riley** (53.37), who fell short of our efficiency threshold despite a solid base projection. While **Austin Riley** remains a capable hitter, the matchup against the tougher arm of Lodolo (3.83 xERA) pushed his score beneath the likes of **Kyle Karros** (62.96) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (65.59), the latter of whom gains a massive park factor boost today. **Jose Caballero** (69.24) also earns a high-leverage start, largely due to a rare 13% boost from a favorable platoon split against a lefty, cementing his value as a niche upside play.\n\nCaution remains warranted for the bottom of our order. Players like **Dalton Rushing** (53.28) and **Alex Freeland** (48.37) are skating near the lower bound of our start criteria, with both scores heavily reliant on pending lineups. Should **Alex Freeland** drop further in the order than the assumed #9, his score would crater; similarly, **Henry Bolte** (49.63) is currently operating near his floor, though he maintains start status due to park-induced projections. We are bypassing **Travis Bazzana** (39.92) entirely, as he falls beneath our \"Zebras Floor\" due to a poor platoon matchup against Suarez. Monitor the official lineups closely, as any deviation in the #7\u2013#9 slots could force a re-evaluation of our daily cap preservation.",
